WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.380
In the first film, survival fed into R0.

2
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.640
Now watch survival alone: half of this cohort dies every year.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.420
Do the same number die each year?

4
00:00:16.183 --> 00:00:19.263
In the first year, half of the cohort fades to grey.

5
00:00:20.303 --> 00:00:23.043
The grey bar below the axis counts those deaths.

6
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:27.947
The next year, half die again, but half of a smaller group.

7
00:00:29.307 --> 00:00:31.247
Year after year, the grey bars shrink.

8
00:00:32.487 --> 00:00:37.287
A constant chance of death removes the same fraction each year, not the same number.

9
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.050
Plot the share still alive against age, on an ordinary axis.

10
00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:47.350
The points fall steeply at first, then the curve bends and flattens toward zero.

11
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.760
Ecologists usually plot survival on a log axis instead.

12
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.300
On a log axis, every halving is the same step down.

13
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.860
So equal drops mean equal fractions lost.

14
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.410
Watch the same points.

15
00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.050
They slide into a straight line.

16
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.850
A constant chance of death draws a straight line on a log axis.

17
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.550
This shape is called Type II.

18
00:01:14.583 --> 00:01:16.783
Two other shapes sit beside it.

19
00:01:17.903 --> 00:01:21.543
Type I stays high, then plunges, because most deaths come late.

20
00:01:23.003 --> 00:01:26.943
Type III drops steeply, then flattens, because most deaths come early.

21
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:30.493
Here is another cohort on the log axis.

22
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:35.333
Its line stays almost flat, then plunges at old ages.

23
00:01:36.693 --> 00:01:40.233
Is the chance of dying constant, rising, or falling with age?

24
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:53.410
Rising. Few die young, and the chance of dying climbs with age, so the line bends

25
00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:56.630
down late. That is close to Type I.

26
00:01:57.633 --> 00:02:00.633
So does every animal's chance of dying rise with age?

27
00:02:01.873 --> 00:02:07.333
A classic reading of ageing theory said yes, for every multicellular species that

28
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:08.333
breeds repeatedly.

29
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:14.817
Researchers kept more than two thousand small freshwater Hydra in the laboratory, in

30
00:02:14.817 --> 00:02:20.117
twelve cohorts. They checked every animal daily for deaths, and for new buds.

31
00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:26.270
Deaths from culture accidents were not counted as natural deaths.

32
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:31.790
Those animals counted as at risk while they were watched, then left the table.

33
00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.210
That is called censoring.

34
00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:40.650
In ten of the twelve cohorts, the yearly chance of natural death stayed near 6 in

35
00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.690
1,000 (0.006). On the log axis that is a straight, nearly level line.

36
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.350
Budding did not systematically fall with age.

37
00:02:50.433 --> 00:02:54.153
That holds for laboratory cohorts, under that counting rule.

38
00:02:55.313 --> 00:02:59.633
It does not show that Hydra are immortal, or that they live this way in ponds.

39
00:03:00.817 --> 00:03:04.997
So a straight line on a log axis means a constant chance of death.

40
00:03:06.137 --> 00:03:09.557
And a chance of death that rises with age is not universal.
